-- CISSP Domain 1 (Security & Risk Management) expansion, part C of 4.
-- Adds objective 1.8 (BIA/BCP/DRP planning, RTO/RPO/MTD/WRT metrics), 1.9
-- (personnel security: screening, employment/termination agreements,
-- vendor/contractor personnel risk), and 1.10 (quantitative risk mechanics
-- beyond SLE/ARO/ALE basics: control category vs type, appetite vs
-- tolerance, quantitative vs qualitative analysis, inherent/residual/
-- control-gap, risk register/owner, cost-benefit, treatment scenarios).

insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
-- 1.8 core
('ci-f-400','cissp','core',1,'1.8',$$What is Work Recovery Time (WRT), and how does it relate to RTO and MTD?$$,$$WRT is the time needed after systems are technically restored (post-RTO) to validate data, catch up on backlog, and resume full business operations. RTO + WRT must not exceed the MTD - MTD is the outer ceiling both must fit inside.$$),
('ci-f-401','cissp','core',1,'1.8',$$What are the five core steps of a Business Impact Analysis (BIA)?$$,$$1) Identify critical business processes and supporting assets. 2) Determine the impact of their disruption over time. 3) Establish the Maximum Tolerable Downtime (MTD) for each. 4) Derive RTO and RPO targets. 5) Identify the resources and recovery priorities needed to meet them.$$),
('ci-f-402','cissp','core',1,'1.8',$$What impact criteria does a BIA weigh besides direct dollar loss?$$,$$Financial loss (revenue, contractual penalties) plus qualitative factors: reputational/brand damage, regulatory or legal exposure, and safety/life impact. A short outage in one category can still be unacceptable if the qualitative harm is severe.$$),
('ci-f-403','cissp','core',1,'1.8',$$How does a BIA differ from a risk assessment in what it needs to know?$$,$$A BIA is impact-focused and "all-hazards" - it doesn't need to know the likelihood or cause of a disruption, only its consequences over time. A risk assessment instead weighs likelihood x impact for specific threats to decide which risks to prioritize.$$),
('ci-f-404','cissp','core',1,'1.8',$$Why must a BC plan document external dependencies?$$,$$Suppliers, utility providers, telecom/ISP links, and outsourced or cloud/SaaS services are often single points of failure the organization does not control - if any fails, dependent internal processes cannot recover no matter how well internal systems are prepared.$$),
('ci-f-405','cissp','core',1,'1.8',$$What are the typical MTD criticality tiers used to prioritize recovery?$$,$$Roughly: critical (minutes to hours), urgent (24 hours), important (72 hours), normal (7 days), nonessential (30 days). Functions in higher tiers get first claim on recovery resources and the most expensive recovery strategies.$$),
('ci-f-406','cissp','core',1,'1.8',$$On a disruption timeline, how do RPO and RTO differ in direction?$$,$$RPO looks backward from the moment of disruption to the last point of usable data (how much data you can afford to lose). RTO looks forward from the disruption to full restoration (how long you can afford to be down).$$),
('ci-f-407','cissp','core',1,'1.8',$$What must happen before a BCP project can produce a usable plan?$$,$$A project initiation/scope phase: securing visible executive sponsorship, chartering a cross-functional BC planning team representing every business unit, and documenting the plan's scope and assumptions - without this, later analysis lacks authority and coverage.$$),
('ci-f-408','cissp','core',1,'1.8',$$Why does recovery order depend on process interdependency mapping, not just each process's own MTD?$$,$$A process with a short MTD may depend on another system (network, database, authentication) that must be restored first regardless of that dependency's own MTD ranking - interdependency mapping prevents recovering an application before the infrastructure it needs.$$),
('ci-f-409','cissp','core',1,'1.8',$$What is the difference between a Continuity of Operations Plan (COOP) and a BCP?$$,$$COOP is the equivalent continuity term used in (US) government/public-sector agencies, emphasizing preserving essential functions and a clear order of succession/delegation of authority. A BCP is the general private-sector term for keeping critical business functions running.$$),
('ci-f-410','cissp','core',1,'1.8',$$Besides restoring technology, what should a BC strategy include for the interruption period itself?$$,$$Alternate/manual business processes - paper-based order entry, manual approvals, temporary staffing at another location - that let critical work continue at a reduced level while systems are still being restored, rather than stopping entirely.$$),
-- 1.9 core
('ci-f-411','cissp','core',1,'1.9',$$Why should the depth of a pre-employment background check scale with the sensitivity of the position rather than be identical for every role?$$,$$A role with access to finances, privileged systems, or highly sensitive data carries more potential harm if the person is untrustworthy, so it justifies deeper screening (credit history, more extensive criminal/reference checks). Applying the deepest check to every hire wastes resources and delays hiring for low-risk roles; applying too little to high-risk roles leaves real exposure unaddressed.$$),
('ci-f-412','cissp','core',1,'1.9',$$What elements typically make up a standard pre-employment background check?$$,$$Identity verification, criminal history check, education and professional credential verification, prior employment verification, and reference checks - with a credit history check added for roles with financial or fiduciary access.$$),
('ci-f-413','cissp','core',1,'1.9',$$What agreements does an organization typically have a new hire sign before granting system or information access?$$,$$A Non-Disclosure Agreement (protecting confidential/proprietary information), an acceptable use policy acknowledgment, and often an intellectual-property/invention assignment agreement - executed before, not after, access to sensitive systems or data is granted.$$),
('ci-f-414','cissp','core',1,'1.9',$$What is the difference between a friendly and an unfriendly (hostile) termination, from a security standpoint?$$,$$A friendly termination follows the standard offboarding checklist over a notice period. An unfriendly/hostile termination (layoff, firing for cause) requires access to be revoked at or just before the moment the employee is notified - not afterward - since a disgruntled departing employee has both motive and, until revoked, means to retaliate.$$),
('ci-f-415','cissp','core',1,'1.9',$$What should a standard termination/offboarding checklist include?$$,$$Disable or delete system and application accounts, revoke badge/physical access, retrieve company property and devices, remind the departing employee of any ongoing NDA or non-compete obligations, notify security and IT of the departure, and conduct an exit interview.$$),
('ci-f-416','cissp','core',1,'1.9',$$How does a mandatory vacation policy function as a personnel security control?$$,$$Forcing an employee to be away for a continuous period requires someone else to perform their duties, increasing the chance that fraud, error, or unauthorized activity the original employee was concealing (e.g., manipulating records) gets noticed while they are absent.$$),
('ci-f-417','cissp','core',1,'1.9',$$What personnel security requirements apply to vendor, contractor, and consultant staff with access to sensitive systems?$$,$$Third-party personnel accessing sensitive systems or data should be held to the same (or supplementary) background-screening, NDA, and access-control requirements as employees, defined and enforced through the contract/SLA with their employer - access risk doesn't shrink just because the person isn't on the payroll.$$),
('ci-f-418','cissp','core',1,'1.9',$$Why are privileged users, disgruntled employees, and third-party personnel considered the highest-risk population for insider threat?$$,$$They combine legitimate access with either elevated capability (privileged users), motive (disgruntled employees), or reduced organizational visibility/oversight (third-party personnel) - the same properties that make external attackers dangerous, but starting from inside the trust boundary.$$),
('ci-f-419','cissp','core',1,'1.9',$$What is succession planning, and how does it relate to personnel security?$$,$$Identifying and preparing backups for key roles in advance so the organization isn't dependent on one irreplaceable person. It reduces the risk of both accidental single points of human failure (illness, departure) and coercion/insider risk tied to one person holding unique knowledge or access.$$),
('ci-f-420','cissp','core',1,'1.9',$$What is a position sensitivity (or clearance) designation, and what does it drive?$$,$$A formal classification of how much risk a role's access represents, assigned before recruiting begins. It determines the required screening tier (how deep the background check must be) and sometimes ongoing reinvestigation requirements for that role.$$),
('ci-f-421','cissp','core',1,'1.9',$$Why should a Non-Disclosure Agreement be signed before, not after, sensitive information is shared?$$,$$An NDA signed after disclosure cannot un-share information that's already been seen and cannot deter behavior that has already occurred - its deterrent and legal value depends on the recipient having formally agreed to confidentiality obligations before gaining access.$$),
('ci-f-422','cissp','core',1,'1.9',$$What is a conflict-of-interest disclosure requirement, and why is it part of personnel security policy?$$,$$A requirement that employees (and often contractors) disclose outside relationships, financial interests, or roles that could bias their judgment or create incentive for misuse of access. Disclosure lets the organization manage or restrict access proactively rather than discovering a conflict only after it has caused harm.$$),
('ci-f-423','cissp','core',1,'1.9',$$How do HR processes and security controls typically interlock in the personnel security lifecycle?$$,$$HR owns the workflow events (hire, role change, termination) and security defines the access-control actions triggered by each - for example, an HR-recorded termination should automatically trigger account disablement and badge deactivation, rather than relying on a separate, unlinked manual request.$$),
-- 1.10 core
('ci-f-424','cissp','core',1,'1.10',$$What is the difference between a control's category and its functional type?$$,$$Category describes HOW a control is implemented: administrative (policies, training, procedures), technical/logical (encryption, access lists, firewalls), or physical (locks, fences, guards). Type describes WHAT the control does: preventive, detective, corrective, deterrent, compensating, or directive. Any single control has one category and one (or more) type - e.g., a badge reader is a technical, preventive control.$$),
('ci-f-425','cissp','core',1,'1.10',$$What is the "recovery" control type, and how does it differ from "corrective"?$$,$$Some frameworks add recovery as a distinct type from corrective: corrective controls stop ongoing damage and fix the immediate cause (e.g., killing a malicious process), while recovery controls restore full normal operation afterward (e.g., restoring data from backup, failing over to a DR site).$$),
('ci-f-426','cissp','core',1,'1.10',$$What is the difference between risk appetite and risk tolerance?$$,$$Risk appetite is the broad, overall amount and type of risk an organization is willing to pursue or retain in general, set by senior leadership. Risk tolerance is the acceptable variance around a specific objective or metric (e.g., "we tolerate up to 2 hours of downtime per quarter") - appetite is strategic and broad; tolerance is tactical and specific.$$),
('ci-f-427','cissp','core',1,'1.10',$$What is the practical trade-off between quantitative and qualitative risk analysis?$$,$$Quantitative analysis assigns dollar figures (SLE, ARO, ALE) for precise, comparable cost-justification but requires reliable data and more effort. Qualitative analysis uses relative ratings (low/medium/high, a risk matrix) - faster and usable with limited data, but more subjective and harder to directly compare across risks.$$),
('ci-f-428','cissp','core',1,'1.10',$$What is a risk matrix (heat map) used for?$$,$$A qualitative tool that plots each identified risk by likelihood on one axis and impact on the other, visually grouping risks into priority bands (e.g., low/medium/high) to help decide which risks need treatment first without needing precise dollar figures.$$),
('ci-f-429','cissp','core',1,'1.10',$$What is the difference between inherent risk and residual risk?$$,$$Inherent risk is the level of risk that exists before any controls are applied. Residual risk is what remains after controls are implemented. The gap between them represents the risk-reduction the controls actually achieve.$$),
('ci-f-430','cissp','core',1,'1.10',$$What is a "control gap," and how does it relate to residual risk?$$,$$The control gap is the portion of inherent risk that the implemented controls do NOT address - it is effectively the difference between inherent risk and how much a control set reduces it, and it flows directly into (becomes) the residual risk that remains.$$),
('ci-f-431','cissp','core',1,'1.10',$$What is a risk register, and what should each entry contain?$$,$$A centralized log tracking every identified risk, including a description, its assigned risk owner, the chosen treatment (mitigate/transfer/avoid/accept), current status, and review date - it is the operational tool that turns a one-time risk assessment into ongoing risk management.$$),
('ci-f-432','cissp','core',1,'1.10',$$What is a risk owner, and why does every risk register entry need one?$$,$$The specific, named individual accountable for monitoring a given risk and ensuring its treatment decision is carried out. Without a named owner, an identified risk tends to go untreated because no one person is responsible for acting on it.$$),
('ci-f-433','cissp','core',1,'1.10',$$What cost-benefit rule guides whether a proposed control is worth implementing?$$,$$A control's annual cost should not exceed the Annualized Loss Expectancy (ALE) it is intended to reduce - spending more on a safeguard than the risk it mitigates is worth is not economically justified, even if the control is technically effective.$$),
('ci-f-434','cissp','core',1,'1.10',$$What is risk concentration (a single point of failure in risk terms)?$$,$$A situation where multiple safeguards or the mitigation of multiple risks all depend on one component, person, or control - if that single point fails, several risk treatments fail simultaneously, undermining the assumed level of protection across the board.$$),
('ci-f-435','cissp','core',1,'1.10',$$Besides mitigate, transfer, avoid, and accept, what ongoing activity must follow any risk treatment decision?$$,$$Monitoring - because threats, vulnerabilities, and business context change over time, a treatment decision made once must be revisited periodically (via the risk register) rather than treated as permanent.$$),
-- acronyms
('ci-a-160','cissp','acronym',1,'1.8',$$WRT$$,$$Work Recovery Time - the time after systems are restored (post-RTO) needed to validate data and fully resume the business function; RTO + WRT should not exceed the MTD.$$),
('ci-a-161','cissp','acronym',1,'1.8',$$MAO$$,$$Maximum Allowable Outage - synonym for MTD (also called MTPD): the longest a function can be unavailable before the harm becomes unacceptable.$$),
('ci-a-162','cissp','acronym',1,'1.8',$$SDO$$,$$Service Delivery Objective - the minimum acceptable level of service (not full capacity) that must be maintained while operating from an alternate/recovery mode.$$),
('ci-a-163','cissp','acronym',1,'1.8',$$COOP$$,$$Continuity of Operations Plan - the government-sector continuity plan preserving essential functions and the order of succession during a disruption.$$),
('ci-a-164','cissp','acronym',1,'1.9',$$NDA$$,$$Non-Disclosure Agreement - a signed commitment to protect confidential/proprietary information, executed before sensitive access is granted.$$),
('ci-a-165','cissp','acronym',1,'1.9',$$AUP$$,$$Acceptable Use Policy - the rules a user agrees to follow when using organizational systems and data, typically acknowledged at onboarding.$$),
('ci-a-166','cissp','acronym',1,'1.9',$$COI$$,$$Conflict of Interest (disclosure) - a requirement to report outside interests or relationships that could bias judgment or create incentive to misuse access.$$),
('ci-a-167','cissp','acronym',1,'1.10',$$RMF$$,$$Risk Management Framework - a structured, repeatable process (e.g., NIST SP 800-37) for categorizing systems, selecting and implementing controls, assessing them, and continuously monitoring risk.$$),
('ci-a-168','cissp','acronym',1,'1.10',$$ROSI$$,$$Return on Security Investment - a cost-justification metric comparing the risk reduction (loss avoided) a control delivers against what it costs to implement and maintain.$$),
('ci-a-169','cissp','acronym',1,'1.10',$$ERM$$,$$Enterprise Risk Management - an organization-wide, holistic approach to identifying and managing risk across all business units and risk categories, not just information security.$$),
-- feynman
('ci-y-090','cissp','feynman',1,'1.8',$$Explain the relationship among MTD, RTO, and WRT using a worked example.$$,$$Model answer: Think of MTD as the outer deadline the business can't survive missing - say a payments API has an MTD of 6 hours before customer and regulatory harm becomes unacceptable. RTO is how long IT needs to bring the systems themselves back online, say 4 hours. But getting the servers running isn't the same as the business function actually working again: staff still need to verify data integrity, clear a backlog of failed transactions, and confirm reconciliation - that's the WRT, maybe 1.5 hours. Add RTO and WRT together (4 + 1.5 = 5.5 hours) and compare it to the MTD (6 hours): the plan fits, with half an hour of margin. If WRT were 3 hours instead, the same RTO would blow past the MTD even though "the system" came back on time - which is exactly why WRT can't be ignored when validating a recovery plan.$$),
('ci-y-091','cissp','feynman',1,'1.8',$$Explain why a Business Impact Analysis does not need to know how likely a disruption is, while a risk assessment does.$$,$$Model answer: A BIA asks a single question - "if this process stops, what happens, and how does the damage grow the longer it stays stopped?" - regardless of whether the cause is a fire, a ransomware attack, a key vendor going bankrupt, or a pandemic. That's what "all-hazards" means: the impact curve for losing the payroll system is the same whether a flood or a cyberattack caused it, so the BIA doesn't need to weigh which cause is more likely. A risk assessment, by contrast, is trying to decide where to spend limited security budget, and that decision genuinely depends on likelihood - a catastrophic but vanishingly rare threat may rank below a moderate, frequent one. The BIA hands the risk assessment (and the BCP/DRP) its MTD/RTO/RPO targets; the risk assessment then decides which specific threats to that same process are worth treating first.$$),
('ci-y-092','cissp','feynman',1,'1.9',$$Explain why revoking a fired employee's access must happen at (or just before) the termination meeting, not sometime after it.$$,$$Model answer: Before the meeting, the employee doesn't yet know they're being let go, so there's no elevated risk from their access. The moment they're told, that changes instantly - a person who is angry, humiliated, or feels they have nothing left to lose has both motive and, if their credentials are still live, the means to delete data, plant something malicious, or exfiltrate proprietary information, and they can often do it from a phone in the parking lot rather than needing to still be sitting at their desk. "Revoke it afterward" assumes there will be a calm, orderly gap between notification and any harmful action, which is exactly the assumption an unfriendly termination can't safely make. Cutting access at (or a few minutes before) the notification removes that window entirely, which is why HR and security coordinate the timing of an involuntary termination so tightly.$$),
('ci-y-093','cissp','feynman',1,'1.9',$$Explain why the depth of a background check should match the risk of the position rather than being the same for every hire.$$,$$Model answer: Background screening exists to reduce a specific risk - hiring someone whose history suggests they're likely to harm the organization through the access their role provides. That harm potential isn't constant across jobs: a cashier with no purchasing authority and a treasury analyst who can move company funds present very different exposure if either turns out to be untrustworthy. Running the deepest possible check (credit history, extensive reference verification, etc.) on every single hire wastes time and money on roles where it changes nothing, slows down hiring for low-risk positions, and can even raise legal/privacy concerns for checks that aren't job-related. Conversely, applying only a light check to a high-risk role leaves real exposure unexamined. Tying screening depth to a documented position-sensitivity rating keeps the control proportional: strong where the access justifies it, lighter where it doesn't.$$),
('ci-y-094','cissp','feynman',1,'1.10',$$Explain the difference between inherent risk and residual risk, and show where "control gap" fits between them.$$,$$Model answer: Inherent risk is what you're exposed to with nothing in place yet - imagine a database with no encryption, no access controls, and no monitoring; the risk of a breach is about as high as it gets. Residual risk is what's left after you actually build your defenses: add encryption, tight access lists, and logging, and the risk drops substantially, but it never hits zero. The control gap is the label for exactly what separates those two numbers - the specific slice of the original risk your controls didn't reach. If your controls stop external attackers cold but do nothing about a trusted insider abusing legitimate access, that insider scenario is your control gap, and it shows up directly in whatever residual risk remains. Naming the gap explicitly is what lets you decide whether to add another control, transfer that slice of risk, or consciously accept it - rather than residual risk staying a vague, unexplained number.$$);

insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
-- 1.8 questions
('ci-q-400','cissp',1,'1.8','mcq',2,$q$A critical claims-processing function has a Maximum Tolerable Downtime (MTD) of 8 hours. After an outage, IT restores the application and database in 5 hours, and staff then need 2 hours to manually reconcile the backlog of queued claims before normal processing truly resumes. Does this recovery meet the MTD, and why?$q$,$q$["Yes - the 5-hour RTO plus the 2-hour WRT total 7 hours, within the 8-hour MTD","No - the 5-hour RTO alone already fails to leave any margin","Yes, because WRT is not counted against the MTD","No - WRT only applies to disaster recovery sites, not application outages"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$MTD is the outer ceiling that RTO (technical restoration) plus WRT (business catch-up/validation time) must fit inside. Here RTO (5h) + WRT (2h) = 7h, which is within the 8h MTD - the plan meets tolerance with a 1-hour margin. WRT is exactly the concept that accounts for post-restoration reconciliation time, and it applies to any recovery, not only DR-site failovers.$q$),
('ci-q-401','cissp',1,'1.8','mcq',1,$q$A company's internal wiki, used for informal documentation, could be unavailable for up to 30 days with no material business impact. Which MTD criticality tier does this function fall into?$q$,$q$["Nonessential","Urgent","Important","Critical"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The nonessential tier covers functions tolerating roughly 30 days of downtime with minimal impact. Urgent (~24 hours), important (~72 hours), and critical (minutes to hours) tiers apply to functions whose loss causes escalating, faster-onset harm.$q$),
('ci-q-402','cissp',1,'1.8','mcq',2,$q$A BIA team is evaluating the impact of losing the order-fulfillment system but is told not to worry about how likely such an outage is, only what would happen if it occurred. What does this instruction reflect about the nature of a BIA?$q$,$q$["A BIA is an impact-focused, all-hazards analysis that does not require knowing the probability or cause of disruption","A BIA is invalid without a documented probability for each threat","A BIA and a risk assessment are the same analysis performed twice","Probability is always assumed to be 100% in a BIA"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The BIA deliberately separates impact analysis from likelihood - it asks "what happens if this function is unavailable" regardless of cause, which is why it's called all-hazards. A risk assessment is the complementary analysis that weighs likelihood x impact per specific threat to prioritize which risks to treat.$q$),
('ci-q-403','cissp',1,'1.8','mcq',2,$q$During BC planning, a team documents that its shipping process depends entirely on a single regional freight carrier and a single electrical utility feed, neither of which the company controls. What BC planning activity does this represent?$q$,$q$["Identifying external dependencies that could interrupt the process regardless of internal readiness","Calculating the Annualized Loss Expectancy for the shipping process","Performing a penetration test on the carrier's systems","Classifying the shipping data according to its sensitivity"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Documenting reliance on external parties or infrastructure (a carrier, a utility feed, a cloud provider, an ISP) the organization doesn't control is exactly what external-dependency identification in BC planning covers - these are single points of failure that can defeat even a well-prepared internal recovery.$q$),
('ci-q-404','cissp',1,'1.8','multi',2,$q$A BIA is evaluating the impact of losing a customer-facing payments system. Select the THREE categories of impact it should weigh. (Choose three.)$q$,$q$["Direct financial loss and contractual penalties","Reputational and customer-trust damage","Regulatory or legal exposure","The vendor's marketing calendar for the quarter","Employee satisfaction survey results unrelated to the outage"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1,2]'::jsonb,$q$A BIA weighs the real consequences of disruption: direct financial loss, reputational/trust damage, and regulatory or legal exposure (and, for safety-critical processes, life/safety impact). A vendor's marketing schedule and an unrelated satisfaction survey are not impact criteria tied to the outage.$q$),
('ci-q-405','cissp',1,'1.8','ordering',2,$q$Place these Business Impact Analysis steps in the order they are normally performed.$q$,$q$["Identify critical business processes and supporting assets","Determine the impact of disruption to each process over time","Establish the Maximum Tolerable Downtime (MTD) for each process","Derive RTO and RPO targets from the MTD","Identify resource requirements and recovery priorities"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1,2,3,4]'::jsonb,$q$The BIA starts by scoping what's critical, then measures how disruption impact grows over time, which sets the MTD ceiling; RTO/RPO targets are then derived to fit inside that ceiling, and finally resource needs and recovery order are identified to hit those targets.$q$),
('ci-q-406','cissp',1,'1.8','mcq',3,$q$A company's backup job runs nightly at 02:00. Ransomware encrypts production data at 13:00 the same day, and administrators restore from the prior night's backup. Measured against a documented 4-hour Recovery Point Objective, what does this incident reveal?$q$,$q$["11 hours of data was lost, which exceeds the 4-hour RPO target, so backups must run more frequently","1 hour of data was lost, meeting the RPO target","No data is lost, because RPO only governs downtime, not data loss","13 hours of data was lost, and this satisfies the RPO since a backup existed"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$From the 02:00 backup to the 13:00 encryption is 11 hours of unprotected changes, all lost on restore - far beyond the 4-hour RPO the business documented as tolerable. RPO measures acceptable data loss (not downtime), and meeting it here would require backups at least every 4 hours, such as more frequent snapshots or continuous replication.$q$),
('ci-q-407','cissp',1,'1.8','matching',2,$q$Match each business continuity metric to its definition.$q$,$q${"left":["MTD","RTO","RPO","WRT"],"right":["The longest a function can be down before unacceptable harm","The target time to restore systems/infrastructure after an outage","The maximum tolerable data loss, measured backward in time","The additional time after systems are restored needed to fully resume the business function"]}$q$::jsonb,'[0,1,2,3]'::jsonb,$q$MTD is the outer ceiling for total downtime; RTO is the systems-restoration target within it; RPO measures how much data loss (by time) is tolerable; WRT is the catch-up/validation time after systems are back before the business function is truly operating normally again. RTO + WRT must not exceed MTD.$q$),
('ci-q-408','cissp',1,'1.8','mcq',3,$q$A recovery team plans to restore the order-processing application first because it has the shortest MTD, but the application cannot function until the core network and the customer database are available. What does this scenario illustrate about setting recovery priority?$q$,$q$["Recovery order must account for process interdependencies, not each process's MTD in isolation","MTD alone always determines correct recovery sequencing","Databases should always be restored last to protect data integrity","Network restoration is never a BC planning concern"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Recovery sequencing has to follow the dependency chain - restoring an application before the network and database it relies on wastes time and can't succeed. Interdependency mapping during BC planning ensures the true recovery order (often network, then data services, then applications) is followed even when it means an urgent function waits briefly for a dependency.$q$),
('ci-q-409','cissp',1,'1.8','mcq',1,$q$A federal government agency's continuity documentation emphasizes preserving "essential functions" and a defined "order of succession" for leadership during a disruption. What is this type of plan most specifically called?$q$,$q$["A Continuity of Operations Plan (COOP)","A Disaster Recovery Plan (DRP)","A Service Level Agreement (SLA)","A vulnerability management plan"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$COOP is the continuity terminology specific to government/public-sector agencies, built around essential-function preservation and succession of authority. DRP is the IT-restoration subset of continuity planning; the other two options are unrelated agreement/program types.$q$),
('ci-q-410','cissp',1,'1.8','mcq',2,$q$While its e-commerce platform is being restored, a retailer has store staff take customer orders on paper forms and process payments manually until systems are back online. What does this represent in BC planning terms?$q$,$q$["An alternate/manual business process that sustains reduced operations during the interruption window","A violation of the documented RPO","A form of risk avoidance","An unnecessary step once a DRP exists"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$BC strategies aren't limited to restoring technology - they also define manual or alternate ways of working that let critical functions continue at reduced capacity while systems recover, which is exactly what the paper-based workaround provides here.$q$),
('ci-q-411','cissp',1,'1.8','mcq',1,$q$Before any BIA data is gathered, what should be the FIRST step in launching a Business Continuity Plan project?$q$,$q$["Obtain executive sponsorship and charter a cross-functional BC planning team with a defined scope","Purchase a hot site contract","Publish the finished plan to all employees","Conduct a penetration test of the production network"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A BCP project needs visible management commitment and a representative planning team before analysis begins, since the scope, authority, and resourcing for everything that follows depend on it. Site contracts, publishing, and testing all come later, after the BIA and strategy phases.$q$),
('ci-q-412','cissp',1,'1.8','multi',2,$q$Select the THREE items that are core components of a mature business continuity program's output. (Choose three.)$q$,$q$["Documented recovery strategies for each critical process","An alternate site or recovery location plan","A communications plan for notifying staff and stakeholders","The company's annual marketing budget","A general employee performance review template"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1,2]'::jsonb,$q$A BC program's deliverables include recovery strategies, an alternate-site/recovery plan, and a communications plan to reach staff, customers, and stakeholders during a disruption. Marketing budgets and performance-review templates are unrelated to continuity planning.$q$),
('ci-q-413','cissp',1,'1.8','mcq',3,$q$During a failover to its alternate processing site, an organization can only handle 40% of normal transaction volume - a level documented in advance as the minimum acceptable during recovery operations. What term describes this documented minimum service level?$q$,$q$["Service Delivery Objective (SDO)","Recovery Point Objective (RPO)","Maximum Tolerable Downtime (MTD)","Annualized Loss Expectancy (ALE)"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The Service Delivery Objective defines the minimum acceptable level of service - short of full capacity - that must be sustained while operating in alternate/recovery mode. RPO concerns data loss, MTD concerns downtime duration, and ALE is a risk-quantification figure, none of which describe a service-capacity floor.$q$),
-- 1.9 questions
('ci-q-414','cissp',1,'1.9','mcq',3,$q$A manager is about to inform an employee that they are being terminated for cause, and the employee is known to be volatile. What should happen to that employee's system access?$q$,$q$["It should be revoked at or immediately before the termination conversation, not afterward","It should remain active until the end of the workday so the employee can finish tasks","It should be revoked only after the employee has left the building on their own schedule","It does not need to be revoked if the employee signed an NDA at hire"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$In an unfriendly/hostile termination, access is cut at or just before the notification itself - waiting until "later" gives a motivated, still-credentialed person a window to retaliate, exfiltrate data, or sabotage systems. A signed NDA is a legal deterrent, not a substitute for timely access revocation.$q$),
('ci-q-415','cissp',1,'1.9','mcq',2,$q$An organization requires a credit history check for every new hire, including warehouse staff with no financial system access and no purchasing authority. What principle does this practice violate?$q$,$q$["Screening depth should be proportional to the risk/sensitivity of the position","Background checks are illegal for non-management roles","Credit checks are never appropriate for any position","Reference checks should replace credit checks entirely"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Screening should scale with the access and risk a role carries; a credit check is relevant for roles with financial or fiduciary responsibility, not roles with no such exposure. Applying the deepest possible screening universally wastes resources without reducing risk that isn't there.$q$),
('ci-q-416','cissp',1,'1.9','mcq',2,$q$A financial controller has never taken more than a single day off in three years and resists any suggestion of extended leave. From a personnel security standpoint, what risk does this raise, and what control addresses it?$q$,$q$["It may be concealing fraud or irregularities; mandatory vacation forces another person to perform the role and surfaces problems","It indicates strong dedication and requires no security response","It is purely an HR morale issue with no security relevance","It suggests the role needs a deeper background check, not a vacation policy"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Persistent refusal to take leave is a classic fraud-detection red flag, because ongoing manipulation (e.g., of financial records) often requires the perpetrator's continued personal involvement to stay hidden. Mandatory vacation forces someone else into the role, increasing the odds that irregularities surface.$q$),
('ci-q-417','cissp',1,'1.9','mcq',1,$q$A contractor from a staffing agency will have the same access to the production database as a full-time employee on the team. What personnel security requirement should apply to that contractor?$q$,$q$["The same (or supplementary) background screening, NDA, and access controls as an employee in an equivalent role","No screening, since the staffing agency is contractually liable for the contractor's conduct","Only a verbal confirmation of trustworthiness from the agency","Screening only if the contractor will be on-site more than one year"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Access risk is determined by what a person can reach, not by their employment classification - a contractor with employee-equivalent access should be held to employee-equivalent (or supplementary) screening, confidentiality, and access-control requirements, typically specified in the staffing contract/SLA.$q$),
('ci-q-418','cissp',1,'1.9','multi',3,$q$Select the THREE items that belong on a standard employee termination/offboarding checklist. (Choose three.)$q$,$q$["Disable system and application accounts","Retrieve company-owned devices and badges","Notify security and IT of the departure","Update the company's public marketing website","Schedule the departing employee's replacement's performance review"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1,2]'::jsonb,$q$Offboarding centers on cutting off access and reclaiming assets: disabling accounts, retrieving devices/badges, and notifying security/IT so downstream controls (e.g., access reviews) fire. Marketing updates and a future hire's performance review are unrelated to the departure itself.$q$),
('ci-q-419','cissp',1,'1.9','multi',1,$q$Select the THREE elements typically included in a pre-employment background check. (Choose three.)$q$,$q$["Criminal history check","Verification of prior employment","Verification of education/credentials","The candidate's personal social media aesthetic preferences","The candidate's political party affiliation"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1,2]'::jsonb,$q$Standard screening verifies facts relevant to trustworthiness and qualifications: criminal history, employment history, and education/credentials (plus credit history for financially sensitive roles). Personal aesthetic preferences and political affiliation are not legitimate, job-related screening criteria.$q$),
('ci-q-420','cissp',1,'1.9','ordering',2,$q$Place these stages of the personnel security lifecycle in the order they normally occur for an employee.$q$,$q$["Background screening appropriate to position sensitivity","Signing employment agreements (NDA, acceptable use policy)","Onboarding with least-privilege account provisioning","Ongoing monitoring and periodic access review","Termination and access deprovisioning"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1,2,3,4]'::jsonb,$q$Personnel security follows the employment lifecycle: screen before hire based on role risk, have the candidate sign the relevant agreements, provision access at onboarding with least privilege, monitor and periodically review access during employment, and deprovision access at termination.$q$),
('ci-q-421','cissp',1,'1.9','matching',3,$q$Match each personnel security element to its purpose.$q$,$q${"left":["NDA","Acceptable Use Policy (AUP)","Pre-employment background check","Succession planning"],"right":["Legally binds the individual to protect confidential/proprietary information","Defines acceptable behavior when using organizational systems and data","Verifies a candidate's history and trustworthiness before granting access","Prepares a backup for a key role to avoid a single point of human failure"]}$q$::jsonb,'[0,1,2,3]'::jsonb,$q$An NDA protects confidentiality obligations; an AUP sets expected behavior on company systems; a background check verifies a candidate before access is granted; succession planning removes over-reliance on any one person holding unique access or knowledge.$q$),
('ci-q-422','cissp',1,'1.9','mcq',1,$q$An organization has no documented backup for its sole database administrator, who holds unique institutional knowledge of the production environment. What personnel security practice most directly addresses this exposure?$q$,$q$["Succession planning","Mandatory vacation only","A stricter non-compete agreement","A deeper background check on the current administrator"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Succession planning identifies and prepares a backup for critical, hard-to-replace roles precisely to avoid this kind of single point of human failure. A vacation policy might momentarily surface the gap but doesn't fix it; a non-compete or a background check on the existing DBA does nothing to address the lack of a trained backup.$q$),
('ci-q-423','cissp',1,'1.9','mcq',2,$q$A vendor asks to review a company's confidential system architecture "before" formally signing a Non-Disclosure Agreement, promising to sign it "right after" the meeting. What is the risk with this sequencing?$q$,$q$["Once shared, the information cannot be un-disclosed, so signing afterward provides no real protection for what was already revealed","There is no risk, since the vendor's intent to sign is a binding verbal contract","NDAs are only enforceable if signed in front of a notary, so timing is irrelevant either way","Sharing before signing is standard best practice to build trust with vendors"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An NDA's value comes from establishing a binding confidentiality obligation before disclosure - once information is shared, a later signature cannot retroactively protect what the recipient has already seen and cannot deter the disclosure that already happened. Sensitive information should never be shared ahead of an executed NDA.$q$),
('ci-q-424','cissp',1,'1.9','mcq',3,$q$Which population is generally considered the highest insider-threat risk, combining legitimate access with elevated capability, motive, or reduced oversight?$q$,$q$["Privileged users, disgruntled employees, and third-party personnel with access","Newly hired entry-level staff with no system access yet","Customers using a public-facing website","Anonymous external attackers with no credentials"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Insider-threat risk concentrates where legitimate access meets elevated capability (privileged users), motive (disgruntled employees), or reduced visibility (third-party personnel) - all already inside the trust boundary. External anonymous attackers and access-less new hires are lower insider-threat concerns by definition.$q$),
('ci-q-425','cissp',1,'1.9','mcq',2,$q$An employee is assigned to evaluate vendor bids for a contract, and one bidder is a company owned by the employee's spouse. What should the employee be required to do under personnel security policy?$q$,$q$["Disclose the conflict of interest so the organization can manage or restrict their involvement","Say nothing, since a spouse's company is not a conflict of interest","Award the contract to the spouse's company to avoid awkwardness","Resign from the organization immediately"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Conflict-of-interest policy requires disclosing relationships or interests that could bias judgment or create incentive to misuse access or authority, so the organization can proactively manage the situation (e.g., recusal) rather than discover the conflict only after harm occurs.$q$),
('ci-q-426','cissp',1,'1.9','mcq',1,$q$Two open positions require different levels of pre-employment screening: one is an entry-level retail cashier and the other is a systems administrator with root access to production databases. What determines the difference in screening depth between the two roles?$q$,$q$["Each role's documented position sensitivity/clearance designation, tied to its access risk","Alphabetical order of the job titles","Whichever candidate applied first receives lighter screening","Screening depth should always be identical regardless of role"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Position sensitivity (or clearance) designation formally classifies how much risk a role's access represents and is set before recruiting begins - it, not job title order or application timing, determines how deep the required background screening must be.$q$),
('ci-q-427','cissp',1,'1.9','mcq',3,$q$An employee resigns with two weeks' notice on good terms and continues working through their notice period with normal access. Which type of termination process applies, and what does it typically involve?$q$,$q$["A friendly termination, following the standard offboarding checklist at the end of the notice period","An unfriendly termination, requiring immediate access revocation and an escort out","No termination process is needed since the employee resigned voluntarily","A criminal investigation, since all resignations are treated as hostile by default"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A voluntary, amicable departure on good terms is a friendly termination - access and property are handled through the standard offboarding checklist at the end of the notice period, unlike an unfriendly/hostile termination, which requires immediate, simultaneous access revocation.$q$),
('ci-q-428','cissp',1,'1.9','mcq',3,$q$A company's HR system automatically notifies the identity-management platform the moment an employee's record is marked "terminated," which immediately disables that employee's accounts without requiring a separate manual IT ticket. What personnel-security principle does this design reflect?$q$,$q$["Integrating HR events with security deprovisioning so access changes are triggered directly by the authoritative employment record, not a separate manual process","A violation of separation of duties, since HR should never affect IT systems","An unnecessary control, since manual deprovisioning tickets are always faster","A workaround required only because background checks were skipped"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Tying deprovisioning directly to the authoritative HR termination event closes the gap and delay inherent in a separate manual request, reducing the window an orphaned account could be misused. This reflects good interlock between HR processes and security controls, not a separation-of-duties violation - HR triggers the event, but the identity system, not HR staff, performs the technical action.$q$),
-- 1.10 questions
('ci-q-429','cissp',1,'1.10','mcq',1,$q$A data center requires a badge to unlock the door to the server room before anyone can enter. Which functional control type does the badge reader represent?$q$,$q$["Preventive","Detective","Corrective","Compensating"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A preventive control stops unauthorized action before it happens - here, blocking entry to anyone without a valid badge. Detective controls identify an event after or as it occurs, corrective controls fix damage afterward, and compensating controls substitute for an infeasible primary control.$q$),
('ci-q-430','cissp',1,'1.10','mcq',1,$q$New hires complete mandatory security awareness training as a condition of employment. What control CATEGORY does this represent?$q$,$q$["Administrative","Technical","Physical","Corrective"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Administrative controls are policies, procedures, and training that govern behavior - awareness training falls squarely into this category. (Its functional type is preventive/deterrent, but the question asks about category: administrative, technical, or physical.)$q$),
('ci-q-431','cissp',1,'1.10','matching',2,$q$Match each control to its PRIMARY functional type.$q$,$q${"left":["A perimeter fence around a facility","An intrusion detection system alert","Restoring a server from backup after a ransomware attack","A written security policy requiring all staff to complete training"],"right":["Preventive","Detective","Corrective","Directive"]}$q$::jsonb,'[0,1,2,3]'::jsonb,$q$A fence physically stops entry (preventive); an IDS alert identifies malicious activity already occurring (detective); restoring from backup repairs the damage after an incident (corrective); a policy that mandates behavior guides what people must do (directive).$q$),
('ci-q-432','cissp',1,'1.10','mcq',2,$q$A company states it is "willing to accept moderate cybersecurity risk in exchange for faster product releases" as a board-level statement, while a separate engineering metric specifies "no more than 30 minutes of unplanned downtime per month is acceptable for the payments API." What do these two statements represent, respectively?$q$,$q$["Risk appetite, then risk tolerance","Risk tolerance, then risk appetite","Both are examples of risk appetite","Both are examples of residual risk"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The board-level statement about the broad amount of risk the organization is willing to pursue is risk appetite (strategic, general). The specific numeric threshold for one metric (downtime minutes) is risk tolerance (tactical, bounded to a specific objective).$q$),
('ci-q-433','cissp',1,'1.10','mcq',3,$q$A security team has limited historical loss data and needs a fast, workable prioritization of dozens of newly identified risks for an upcoming steering committee meeting. Which analysis approach is most appropriate for this situation?$q$,$q$["Qualitative analysis using a risk matrix (likelihood x impact ratings)","Quantitative analysis calculating precise SLE/ARO/ALE for every risk","No analysis is needed since committee meetings do not require prioritization","A penetration test of every asset in scope"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$With limited data and time pressure, qualitative analysis (relative likelihood/impact ratings plotted on a risk matrix) delivers a fast, usable prioritization. Quantitative analysis is more precise but needs reliable data and more effort than is available here.$q$),
('ci-q-434','cissp',1,'1.10','multi',1,$q$Select the THREE fields that belong in a well-formed risk register entry. (Choose three.)$q$,$q$["A description of the identified risk","The named risk owner accountable for it","The chosen treatment decision (mitigate/transfer/avoid/accept)","The company's quarterly marketing spend","The office seating chart"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1,2]'::jsonb,$q$A risk register entry tracks the risk itself, who owns it, and how it's being treated (plus status and review date). Marketing spend and seating charts have no bearing on tracking and managing an identified risk.$q$),
('ci-q-435','cissp',1,'1.10','mcq',3,$q$An asset's inherent risk (before any controls) is assessed at a high level. After implementing encryption, access controls, and monitoring, the residual risk drops to a low level, but a gap analysis shows the controls do not address the risk of a trusted insider with legitimate access misusing data. What does this uncovered exposure represent?$q$,$q$["A control gap - the portion of inherent risk the implemented controls do not address","The full inherent risk, meaning the controls accomplished nothing","A risk that no longer needs to be tracked since other controls exist","Proof that the residual risk calculation was performed incorrectly"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The control gap is precisely the slice of inherent risk that existing controls fail to reduce - here, insider misuse by someone with legitimate access. It doesn't mean the controls achieved nothing (residual risk did drop for other threats); it means this specific exposure needs its own additional treatment.$q$),
('ci-q-436','cissp',1,'1.10','mcq',3,$q$A proposed control would cost $15,000 per year to implement and maintain, and it fully mitigates a risk whose Annualized Loss Expectancy is $8,000. From a cost-benefit standpoint, what is the most defensible decision?$q$,$q$["Do not implement the control as designed - its cost exceeds the loss it prevents; look for a cheaper option or accept the risk","Implement the control regardless of cost, since any risk reduction is worth pursuing","Double the control's budget to guarantee it works","Ignore the ALE figure since cost-benefit analysis does not apply to security controls"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A control that costs more annually than the loss it prevents ($15,000 vs. $8,000 ALE) is not economically justified - the organization would spend more defending the risk than the risk is worth. The rational choices are a cheaper compensating control or formally accepting the risk.$q$),
('ci-q-437','cissp',1,'1.10','ordering',2,$q$Order these steps of the risk management process from first to last.$q$,$q$["Identify assets, threats, and vulnerabilities","Analyze and calculate risk (e.g., SLE, ARO, ALE)","Evaluate and select a risk treatment option","Implement the chosen treatment","Monitor and periodically reassess the risk"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1,2,3,4]'::jsonb,$q$Risk management starts by identifying what's at risk and what threatens it, then quantifies/analyzes that risk, selects a treatment, implements it, and finally monitors continuously since risk conditions change over time - it is a cycle, not a one-time project.$q$),
('ci-q-438','cissp',1,'1.10','mcq',1,$q$A newly identified risk is entered into the risk register with a full description and a chosen treatment plan, but no individual is assigned to track or act on it. What is the most likely consequence?$q$,$q$["The risk tends to go untreated, since no one person is accountable for carrying out the decision","Nothing changes, since a treatment plan alone guarantees action","The risk automatically transfers to senior management","The register entry becomes invalid and must be deleted"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Without a named risk owner, accountability for actually executing and following up on the treatment decision is diffuse - risk register entries without an owner routinely stall, which is why assigning an owner is considered essential to every entry.$q$),
('ci-q-439','cissp',1,'1.10','mcq',2,$q$Before any safeguards exist, an asset carries a risk level of "high." After compensating and technical controls are implemented, the documented risk level drops to "low," which the organization formally accepts. Which two risk concepts does this scenario illustrate, in order?$q$,$q$["Inherent risk, then residual risk","Residual risk, then inherent risk","Risk appetite, then risk tolerance","Total risk, then risk avoidance"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The "high" level before any safeguards is inherent risk; the "low" level remaining after controls are applied and formally accepted is residual risk. This scenario doesn't describe appetite/tolerance statements or the avoidance treatment option.$q$),
('ci-q-440','cissp',1,'1.10','mcq',1,$q$A manufacturer discontinues an unprofitable product line specifically because the associated safety-liability risk cannot be reduced to an acceptable level through any available control. Which risk treatment does this represent?$q$,$q$["Avoidance","Mitigation","Transfer","Acceptance"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Avoidance eliminates the risk by eliminating the activity that creates it - here, discontinuing the product line entirely rather than continuing to operate with unacceptable residual risk. This differs from mitigation (reducing risk while continuing the activity), transfer (shifting impact to a third party), and acceptance (retaining the risk as-is).$q$),
('ci-q-441','cissp',1,'1.10','matching',3,$q$Match each scenario to the risk treatment option it represents.$q$,$q${"left":["Deploying multi-factor authentication to reduce the likelihood of account takeover","Outsourcing payment processing to a PCI-compliant processor who contractually bears the compliance risk","Discontinuing a high-risk legacy product line entirely","Documenting management's formal decision to continue running a low-risk legacy system without further investment"],"right":["Mitigation","Transfer","Avoidance","Acceptance"]}$q$::jsonb,'[0,1,2,3]'::jsonb,$q$MFA reduces the likelihood of a specific bad outcome (mitigation). Outsourcing to a processor who contractually assumes compliance risk shifts impact to a third party (transfer). Discontinuing the product line removes the risk-causing activity entirely (avoidance). A documented, informed decision to retain a low risk without further action is acceptance.$q$);
